+# Deterministic dirty-page reuse fixture
+
+`check.py OUTPUT` compiles the actual `tests/xbe/zero-reuse/main.c` with bounded
+allocation, physical-address, IRQL and TAP adapters. It verifies all 54 original
+checks, dirty/sentinel bytes, complete pool-fallback cleanup, and original IRQL
+restoration before reporting on success and injected failures. Entry IRQL 0 and 1
+are tested. The adapter injects a page returned by a finishing thread during the
+unguarded free/reallocate interval, or defers that return until the new guard
+ends. `--guest OLD_MAIN --expect-old` demonstrates that this interleaving breaks
+only the old exact-PFN assertion; byte and sentinel checks still pass.
+
+The guest warms the system-VA page table before the controlled allocation tests.
+One guard covers each iteration from initial allocation through cleanup; there
+are no TAP calls or optional debugger calls inside it. The guest rejects the
+nonpaged-pool fallback before any partial free or physical-address lookup.
+The normal path is `MmAllocateSystemMemory` → `NxkSysVaAllocate` → spinlock/PFN
+allocation/zeroing, and `MmFreeSystemMemory` → `NxkSysVaFree`. Those paths do not
+wait. The public allocation API may internally log if the aperture allocation
+fails; that inherited diagnostic remains unchanged. The test records failure,
+cleans up and restores IRQL before its own fatal reporting.
+
+This host fixture models the interference contract. It does not replace native
+64/128 MiB release/debug guest execution, prove which PFN the old failing native
+run used, or claim a runtime allocator repair.
